# Testing and Verification Rules

Before implementation:

- Inspect the repository for existing test frameworks, test directories, scripts, CI checks, lint commands, type checks, build commands, and validation tools.
- Identify which existing verification commands are relevant to the approved change.
- Do not invent repository commands that do not exist.
- Do not introduce a new testing framework unless the approved specification requires it.

When behavior changes:

- Add or update tests when an applicable test system exists.
- Prefer tests that verify observable behavior and contracts.
- Do not write tests that merely reproduce implementation details.
- Include relevant success, failure, boundary, and regression cases where applicable.

When fixing a bug:

- Reproduce the reported failure before changing behavior when reasonably possible.
- Prefer adding a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after the fix when an applicable test system exists.
- Do not change unrelated behavior while fixing the reported defect.

Existing tests:

- Do not delete, disable, skip, weaken, or rewrite existing tests solely to make a change pass.
- Do not reduce assertions or coverage merely to accommodate an implementation.
- If an existing test conflicts with an approved specification, report the conflict instead of silently changing the test.

Verification order:

- Run the most relevant targeted checks first.
- After targeted checks pass, run broader repository-defined checks when available and relevant.
- Run lint, type-check, build, schema validation, or other repository-defined verification when applicable.
- Inspect the final diff after verification.

Reporting:

- Report the exact verification commands that were actually run.
- Report observed pass or fail results.
- Never report a command as passing if it was not executed.
- If no applicable automated tests or verification commands exist, explicitly report that none were found.

Stop and report BLOCKED when:

- A required verification command fails and cannot be fixed within the approved scope.
- Required tests cannot run because of a repository, environment, dependency, or infrastructure problem.
- Passing the task would require weakening or bypassing existing tests.
- Verification reveals behavior outside the approved contract has changed.
